  {
   "etapa": "explicar",
   "piezas": [
    {
     "tipo": "explicacion",
     "titulo": "Inanimate objects in the accusative",
     "texto": [
      "What you see, buy or read as a direct object goes in the accusative. Inanimate masculine and neuter nouns usually keep the nominative: **Я вижу дом** (I see a house), **Я покупаю шоколад** (I buy chocolate), **Я читаю письмо** (I read a letter). Feminine nouns in **-а** change to **-у**: **книга** → **книгу**, **газета** → **газету**.",
      "Typical verbs: **видеть** (to see), **покупать** (to buy), **читать** (to read), **любить** (to love, to be fond of)."
     ],
     "tutora": ""
    },
    {
     "tipo": "explicacion",
     "titulo": "Living beings in the accusative",
     "texto": [
      "In the singular masculine animate nouns, the accusative is the same as the genitive: **брат** → **брата**, **друг** → **друга**, **кот** → **кота**. In feminine nouns in **-а**, **-а** changes to **-у**: **сестра** → **сестру**, **собака** → **собаку**. In Russian, people and animals are considered animate.",
      "Animate includes people and animals. For inanimate objects, the masculine accusative usually matches the nominative: Я вижу дом, стол."
     ],
     "tutora": ""
    },
